 "answer": {
  "answer": "The study did not find the 5 cases: no setting was confirmed with a failure rate surely above one half, which matches the goal verdict. Two settings came close. In 1c45ce3a, with cars 0.5 s earlier at recorded speed (S30), most runs failed and the range stops just short of the bar. In 09a95ffa, with cars 2 s later at 0.75x speed (S16), most runs failed but the range is wider. In nearly all of these failures the policy was not clearly at fault. The ego slowed for no visible reason or drifted about 2 m left, fell behind its recorded position, and a replayed car that cannot react came up from behind and hit its side or front corner. So these settings may show what non-reactive replay traffic does to a slow ego, more than a cut-in the policy failed to handle. No failures were flagged as possible rendering artifacts.",
  "findings": [
   {
    "claim": "The strongest candidate is 1c45ce3a with cars shifted 0.5 s earlier at 1.0x speed. Most runs failed, but the 90% range still reaches just below one half, so it is not confirmed. In every failure the ego stayed in its lane but kept slowing with no close car ahead, ended up well behind its recorded position, and replayed car 24 came up on the right and sideswiped it or swept into its front-right corner. The policy's fault is unclear.",
    "settings": [
     "S30"
    ]
   },
   {
    "claim": "The second candidate is 09a95ffa with cars shifted 2 s later at 0.75x speed. Most runs failed, but the range is too wide to clear the bar. In these failures the ego slowed and sat about 2 m left of the recorded path, often straddling the lane line. Replayed car 1 clipped its rear and then its right side; in one run the ego steered right, back into car 1. The policy's fault is unclear.",
    "settings": [
     "S16"
    ]
   },
   {
    "claim": "Other settings on these two scenes did not fail in the runs made: other timings and speeds in 09a95ffa, and earlier or faster or slower cars in 1c45ce3a. Most were run only once, so this is a hint and not evidence that they are safe.",
